{
 "lead": "Activation functions turn a neuron's weighted sum into a signal that can be passed to the next layer. Loss functions compare the network's output with the true target and tell the optimizer how far off the prediction is. Together they give a neural network the ability to learn complex patterns from data, whether the task is image classification, fraud detection, or predicting electricity demand during a Chennai power cut.",
 "prereq": "You need: understanding of weighted sums from <a href=\"#multilayer-feedforward-networks-and-hidden-layers\">Multilayer feedforward networks and hidden layers</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Sigmoid and Tanh",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The sigmoid function maps any real input z to the interval (0,1). It is defined as σ(z)=1/(1+exp(-z)). Because its output can be interpreted as a probability, sigmoid is used in binary classification heads."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "σ(z) = 1 ÷ (1 + exp(-z))",
     "where": "z is the pre‑activation value (weighted sum plus bias); exp is the exponential function."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The hyperbolic tangent (tanh) is a scaled version of sigmoid that outputs in the range (-1,1). It is defined as tanh(z)= (exp(z)-exp(-z)) ÷ (exp(z)+exp(-z))."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "tanh(z) = (exp(z) - exp(-z)) ÷ (exp(z) + exp(-z))",
     "where": "z is the pre‑activation value."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "Derivative of sigmoid: σ'(z)=σ(z)·(1-σ(z))",
      "Derivative of tanh: tanh'(z)=1- tanh(z)²"
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Compute z = Σ w_i·x_i + b.",
      "Apply σ(z) or tanh(z) to obtain the activation a.",
      "During back‑propagation, multiply the upstream gradient by the derivative shown above."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "ReLU and Softmax",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU) is defined as ReLU(z)=max(0, z). It is zero for negative inputs and linear for positive inputs, which speeds up training and reduces vanishing‑gradient problems."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "ReLU(z) = max(0, z)",
     "where": "z is the pre‑activation value."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The Softmax function converts a vector of raw scores z⃗ into a probability distribution p⃗ over K classes."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Softmax(z_i) = exp(z_i) ÷ Σ_{j=1}^{K} exp(z_j)",
     "where": "z_i is the i‑th element of the score vector; the denominator sums over all K classes."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "Derivative of ReLU: ReLU'(z)=1 if z>0 else 0",
      "Derivative of Softmax combined with cross‑entropy simplifies to (p_i - t_i) where t_i is the true label."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Compute raw scores z for each class.",
      "Apply Softmax to obtain probabilities p.",
      "Use p in the cross‑entropy loss."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Loss Functions",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Mean‑squared error (MSE) is used for regression. For a single output a and target t, MSE = (a - t)² ÷ 2. The division by 2 simplifies the gradient to (a - t)."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "MSE = (a - t)² ÷ 2",
     "where": "a is the network output; t is the true value."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Cross‑entropy loss is the standard for classification with Softmax. For a true class vector t and predicted probability vector p, the loss is L = - Σ t_i·log(p_i). When t is one‑hot, this reduces to -log(p_{true})."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Cross‑entropy = - Σ t_i·log(p_i)",
     "where": "log is the natural logarithm; p_i is the predicted probability for class i; t_i ∈ {0,1}."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "MSE works well when errors are symmetric and Gaussian.",
      "Cross‑entropy heavily penalises confident wrong predictions, encouraging better calibrated probabilities."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Choose the appropriate loss for the problem (regression vs classification).",
      "Compute the loss using the formula above.",
      "Back‑propagate the gradient of the loss to update weights."
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
